    "summary": "Jazz‑heavy second set with soaring horns, killer Keith solo and tight jams",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a horn‑laden, high‑energy flow highlighted by the Truckin' > Eyes Of The World > Let Me Sing Your Blues Away suite and a soaring Let It Grow. Keith Godchaux’s keyboard work shines throughout, and Jerry Garcia’s vocals are especially compelling. Both soundboard recordings capture the performance with excellent clarity.",
    "review": "September 15, 1973 at the Providence Civic Center finds the Dead deep in their exploratory phase, turning the evening into a jazz‑inflected showcase. After a solid first set, the band launches into a second set that bursts with horn‑driven momentum – the Truckin' > Eyes Of The World > Let Me Sing Your Blues Away sequence rolls out into extended improvisation, while Let It Grow segues seamlessly into Stella Blue, each transition stitched together by bright brass lines. Keith Godchaux delivers a killer solo and provides lush, harmonic texture that lifts the jams, especially during the Weather Report Suite and the soaring Let It Grow. Garcia’s vocals are described as “blow‑me‑away,” and his guitar work sizzles on Bird Song and throughout the extended Truckin' jam. The energy stays high from start to finish, and the soundboard sources preserve every nuance of this horn‑rich, jazz‑heavy performance.",
